window.RG = window.RG || {};
var RG = window.RG;

/* Book study: "Divine Embrace" (part 1: Introduction and Chapters 1-6).
   A popular-level book on union with Christ, the "mirror" reading of the gospel,
   the inclusion of all humanity in Christ, and identity.
   Everything below is paraphrase in our own words; the book's author is not named.
   Scripture, where quoted, is KJV. Where the book's word studies or readings are
   contested by mainstream scholarship, we say so briefly.
   deIntro: the Introduction.
   deCh1: Chapters 1-6, in order. */

RG.deIntro = {
  "distance": "The book answers the lie of distance by insisting that in Jesus God has removed every possible ground for feeling separated: God is nearer to you than your own breath, and oneness with him is something you discover, not something you earn by drawing closer.",
  "delay": "It answers the lie of delay by treating the gospel as news of something already accomplished: the cross and resurrection have already dealt with sin and revealed our innocence, so union is a present reality to wake up to rather than a reward postponed to a future heaven or a future event.",
  "summary": [
    "The book opens by naming the two lies it believes keep religion alive: <strong>distance</strong> (God is far away) and <strong>delay</strong> (the real thing comes later). Its whole message is presented as God's own initiative to cancel every kind of separation. In Jesus, Immanuel arrives as the lover of a humanity that was indifferent and even hostile, and their sins did not deflect his love. Our very calendar, split into before and after Christ, is a witness that something decisive happened in history.",
    "No amount of outer space could ever contain or define God, yet the invisible image and likeness of God has been made known in the one place it could be fully shown: a human life. The point of Jesus' mission, the book says, was never to start a new religion but to reveal and rescue God's image in human form. Jesus is not a model for us to copy from a distance; he is a portrait of who we truly are. Much popular teaching, it argues, turns Jesus into a 'display window' of things we cannot have, whereas God intended him to be a <em>mirror</em> in which we see the blueprint of our own design. The Christ it proclaims is not mainly Christ back in history, or Christ far off in the heavens, or Christ in some future event, but Christ in you (John 14:20; John 10:30; John 14:9). Whatever we thought we knew about God that does not look like Jesus is not God.",
    "Because of this, oneness is not the end-product of earnest striving. It is the discovery of a union God already arranged, and a reconciliation that makes that union real here and now. Paul told idol-worshipping philosophers in Athens that God is not far from any of them (Acts 17:23-31), and the book takes that as true for every person.",
    "The introduction then turns to sacrifice. Every sacrifice in every religion, it says, is a reach for innocence, however brief. In Jesus, God speaks the language of the scapegoat system one last time in order to close it down for good. Psalm 40:6-7 says God never wanted sacrifices. The scandal of the cross is that God does not demand a sacrifice to change his own mind about us; he provides himself as the sacrifice to change <em>our</em> minds about him, about one another and about ourselves, and to remove sin-consciousness, which the book defines as a works-based way of seeing. Even the animal skins given to Adam were not to calm an angry God but to ease Adam's own embarrassment, and to foreshadow the day God would clothe himself in human skin. Our sins led to his death; his resurrection shows we are innocent (Romans 4:25).",
    "Finally it stresses that every human life is equally represented and included in Christ. God is not more 'God with us' to some than to others, so there is no longer an 'us and them'. Nothing you do can make you more God's, and nothing God does can make him more yours. Faith is not the ability to concentrate on God for a few minutes; it is waking up to the fact that God's mind is full of you. Jesus himself is what God believes about the human race. The introduction closes with an image of love from the Song of Songs: the beloved's words and kisses are one and the same."
  ]
};

RG.deCh1 = [
  {
    "id": "ch1-mirror-message",
    "n": 1,
    "title": "The Mirror Message",
    "big": "The cross was God's verdict on a humanity he already treasured, and the gospel is a mirror in which people discover who they have always been in Christ, not a display window of things they must strive to become.",
    "summary": "The chapter tells how the 'mirror message' took shape through four early awakenings in the author's life in South Africa. The first was the realisation that the cross only makes sense in the light of Genesis 1:26: God paid the price because he knew our true worth, not because he pitied our wreckage. A reading of 1 Kings 6:7 then convinced him that the finished work of the cross is the 'quarry' where we were shaped, so God does not need to chip away at us for a lifetime. A story about a captive eagle that only flew when it heard another eagle, and a fresh reading of 2 Corinthians 5:14-17, convinced him that Jesus came to wake people to their freedom and that all were included in his death and resurrection. Finally, the conversion of James, the brother of Jesus, led him to James 1:18-25, where the word of truth works like a mirror showing us the face of our birth.",
    "points": [
      {"head": "The cross starts with Genesis", "text": "We cannot begin to understand the cross until we grasp that humanity was made in God's image and likeness. It was not our pitiful state that moved God to pay such an outrageous price; it was his loving knowledge of what we really are. A lost coin, as the author's mother used to say, never loses its original value."},
      {"head": "The finished work is the quarry", "text": "In 1 Kings 6:7 the temple stones were cut so precisely at the quarry that no hammer or chisel was heard on the building site. Much sincere teaching assumes God must chisel at our character through spouses, bosses, crises and wilderness years until we are finally ready for heaven. The book argues that this turns holiness back into works and time-served; the quarry is the cross, not your marriage, job or trials."},
      {"head": "Underestimating the cross is the only delay", "text": "Any doctrine that distracts from the success of the cross is a waste of time. The only way we delay the glory that follows the cross is to underestimate what happened there. In God's reckoning Jesus died humanity's death once for all, and the rulers of that age did not grasp it, or they would not have crucified the Lord of glory (1 Corinthians 2:8)."},
      {"head": "Unbelief is believing a lie about yourself", "text": "The cross cannot be reversed, but our understanding of our inclusion in it can be veiled, and only with our permission. Paul says unbelieving minds are blinded from seeing God's glory in the face of Jesus as in a mirror (2 Corinthians 4:4; 3:18). Unbelief, in the book's definition, is believing a lie about oneself, as the Israelite spies did when they saw themselves as grasshoppers (Numbers 13:33)."},
      {"head": "A disarmed accuser", "text": "The author was then in a mission that stressed digging up past emotional hurts, spiritual warfare and deliverance, and he found these clashed with the success of the cross. On the cross Jesus' body became the record of human guilt, and by removing guilt God disarmed every accusation and ended the devil's power to blackmail us (Colossians 2:14-15). Preaching a defeated devil back into business is like pretending Pharaoh had not really been overthrown at the Red Sea (Exodus 14:13)."},
      {"head": "Israel's wilderness lesson", "text": "Israel did not die in the desert because their deliverance was second-rate, but because they believed a lie about themselves and underestimated their salvation (Numbers 13:33; Joshua 2:11; Hebrews 2:3). They saw miracles every day for forty years and still died in unbelief. Miracles, the book concludes, prove God's mercy, not the recipients' faith."},
      {"head": "Living stones, already built", "text": "1 Peter 2:2-5 calls us newborn babies who were born again when Jesus rose (1 Peter 1:3) and living stones being built into a spiritual house. Peter does not tell us first to get chiselled into usefulness. The Old Testament pattern was 'do in order to become'; the New Testament pattern is 'be, because of what has been done'. We do not grow into completeness; we grow in our awareness of a completeness we already have."},
      {"head": "The rock you were cut from", "text": "Fittingly it is Peter, 'Mr Rock', who writes about living stones. When Simon recognised Jesus as Messiah, Jesus told him that he too was cut from the same rock, echoing Isaiah 51:1. On that rock Christ builds his <em>ekklesia</em>, and the gates of hades, which the book reads as ignorance, cannot stand against it. Jesus came to reintroduce people to themselves."},
      {"head": "The voice of the free eagle", "text": "Jesus did not come to upgrade the cage of Jewish or any other religion. He came as the human voice of God's image and likeness, to reveal and redeem that image in us (Colossians 1:15; 2:9-10). As James says, we have forgotten what kind of people we are. If the gospel does not sound like the call of a free eagle, it is not the gospel."},
      {"head": "All died, so all were raised", "text": "2 Corinthians 5:14-15 says that one died for all, so all died; and if all were included in his death they were equally included in his resurrection. Therefore Paul no longer knows anyone from a merely human point of view (5:16). Verse 17 begins with 'therefore', which turns the 'if' of 'if anyone is in Christ' from a condition into a conclusion: a question mark becomes an exclamation mark."},
      {"head": "Never exaggerate, only underestimate", "text": "To warn the author against including too many people in the good news is too late. If the gospel does not begin with every person equally valued and included in Christ, it is not good enough. The danger has never been overstating the gospel, only understating it. What happened to humanity in Christ is far greater than what happened in Adam, and it happened without our permission (Ephesians 2:5-6; Colossians 3:1-3)."},
      {"head": "Truth is true before it is believed", "text": "Something is already true before anyone except God believes it; belief does not make a sum correct, though discovering it makes it true for you. That is exactly why the message must be proclaimed urgently. Many people still live blindfolded under the judgment of the 'do-it-yourself tree', though Jesus drew all judgment to himself when he was lifted up and the Lamb took away the world's sin (John 12:32; John 1:29)."},
      {"head": "The test: does it fulfil the law of love?", "text": "The best test of any teaching is whether its fruit fulfils the whole law. Guilt and duty never managed to make anyone love God with everything and their neighbour as themselves. When people see others as God sees them, they find themselves passionate about people."},
      {"head": "Seeing the face of your birth", "text": "James, the brother of Jesus, once knew Jesus only from a human point of view and did not believe in him (John 7:5), but the risen Jesus appeared to him personally (1 Corinthians 15:7). James then wrote that God gave us birth by the word of truth, and that hearing this word is like seeing your own birth-face in a mirror (James 1:18, 23-25). The Old Testament was a display window of promises; the New Testament is a mirror showing our redeemed likeness and innocence. Both the spectator and the participant see the same face; one walks away thinking it too good to be true, the other keeps gazing and is set free to live it."}
    ],
    "illustrations": [
      {"title": "The painting at the auction", "text": "You might be baffled that someone paid an absurd price for a painting, until you learn it was an original Van Gogh. The price reveals the value. In the same way the price God paid at the cross reveals what you are worth: a priceless, authentic masterpiece."},
      {"title": "Treasure in the field", "text": "In Matthew 13:44 a man sells everything to buy a whole field because of the treasure hidden in it. The author saw that we reduce the cross to a sentimental historical event, while the mystery of what God was buying there stays hidden from us."},
      {"title": "Stones finished at the quarry", "text": "While reading 1 Kings 6 (noting with a jolt that verse 1 mentioned the fourth year and second month, exactly how long he had been courting his future wife), verse 7 struck him: Solomon's temple was built without the sound of hammer or chisel because the stones were finished at the quarry. The cross is the quarry where our shaping was completed."},
      {"title": "A wedding-card verse", "text": "The same morning's reading of 1 Peter 2 about living stones built into a spiritual house later found its way onto the couple's wedding card two months on, a playful sign of how these readings shaped both their life and their message."},
      {"title": "The eagle that would not fly", "text": "On their honeymoon in 1979, a nature conservation officer in Mpumalanga told the couple about a captive black eagle being released into the wild. When the crate was opened the bird just sat there, blank-eyed, trapped in an invisible cage. Nothing worked until the cry of a wild eagle sounded overhead, and the bird immediately took off. No flying lessons are needed once the truth is heard; Jesus is that call."},
      {"title": "The same five loaves", "text": "In 1983, newly pastoring a church in White River and anxious about preaching to the same people week after week, the author remembered that Jesus kept multiplying the same five loaves and two fish without ever running out. The numbers five and two nudged him to 2 Corinthians 5, where he saw verse 16 as if for the first time and wept."},
      {"title": "Peter's vision of the sheet", "text": "In Acts 10 God shows Peter a sheet full of unclean animals and declares them clean while they are still alive in their skins, scales, warts and feathers, not after being cleaned and cooked. Outwardly nothing had changed, yet God called them clean. So Peter learns to call no person unclean, because the Lamb has taken away the sin of the world (Psalm 24:1)."},
      {"title": "The photographer and the cormorants", "text": "On a boat trip in his home bay, the author took a celebrated wildlife photographer out to see southern right whales. On the way back, hundreds of Cape cormorants rose together like a ballet over the water, and the photographer shouted that he had got the shot. His joy pictured God's delight when, for the first time, the invisible Creator saw his own image in fragile human form and called it very good."},
      {"title": "Sabbath as satisfaction", "text": "God did not rest on the seventh day because he was tired but because he was satisfied (Genesis 1:31; Zephaniah 3:17). The Sabbath celebrates perfection rather than offering a break from exhaustion. Like a photograph or a word that preserves a moment of beauty for any culture, God's image in us is stored safely whatever happens to the prints."}
    ],
    "words": [
      {"word": "parakuptō (Greek)", "meaning": "Used in James 1:25 for looking into the law of liberty. The book renders it 'mesmerised', from <em>para</em> (close beside, from the source) and <em>kuptō</em> (to stoop down to look closely), picturing someone bending in for a close, absorbed look."},
      {"word": "paramenō (Greek)", "meaning": "From <em>para</em> and <em>menō</em> (to remain). The book takes it as staying captivated under the influence of what one has seen in the mirror, rather than walking away."},
      {"word": "eleutheria (Greek)", "meaning": "Usually translated freedom or liberty. The book stresses the sense of being without obligation, a spontaneous life no longer driven by the written code."},
      {"word": "Petros (Greek)", "meaning": "Peter's name, meaning rock or stone. The book finds it fitting that 'Mr Rock' is the one who writes that believers are living stones cut from the same rock as Christ."},
      {"word": "hades (Greek)", "meaning": "The realm of the dead. The book reads it as 'not seeing' (from a negative plus <em>eidō</em>, to see) and so takes the 'gates of hades' as the power of ignorance. This etymology is traditional but debated, and the usual meaning is simply the underworld."}
    ],
    "questions": [
      {"q": "Does God need a lifetime of trials to make us holy?", "a": "The chapter says no. If sanctification depends on how long we spend in hardship, holiness becomes a matter of works and time. The cross is the quarry where we were shaped, and growth is growth in awareness of what is already complete."},
      {"q": "If everyone was included in Christ, why do so many not live free?", "a": "Because the truth can be veiled from the mind, though only with our consent. Unbelief means believing a lie about oneself, like Israel seeing themselves as grasshoppers, or the eagle staying in an open crate. Hearing the true voice wakes people up."},
      {"q": "Isn't it dangerous to include too many people in the good news?", "a": "The author says the danger runs the other way: underestimating the gospel. 2 Corinthians 5:14 already says all died when one died for all. Critics in traditional theology would answer that Paul's 'all' here must be read alongside his calls to faith and reconciliation (5:20), and that inclusion is applied through faith; the book reads the call to faith as a call to wake up to what is already true."},
      {"q": "Why preach urgently if it is already true?", "a": "Because truth is true before it is believed, but it only becomes your lived experience when you discover it. Many are living blindfolded under judgment that Jesus has already ended, so they need to hear."}
    ],
    "scriptures": ["Genesis 1:26", "Genesis 1:31", "Exodus 14:13", "Numbers 13:33", "Joshua 2:11", "1 Kings 6:1", "1 Kings 6:7", "Psalm 23:2-3", "Psalm 24:1", "Isaiah 51:1", "Jeremiah 1:5", "Hosea 6:2", "Zephaniah 3:17", "Matthew 13:44", "Matthew 16:16-18", "John 1:29", "John 7:5", "John 12:32", "Acts 10", "1 Corinthians 1:30", "1 Corinthians 2:8", "1 Corinthians 15:7", "2 Corinthians 3:18", "2 Corinthians 4:4", "2 Corinthians 4:7", "2 Corinthians 5:14-17", "Ephesians 1:4", "Ephesians 2:5-6", "Colossians 1:6", "Colossians 1:15", "Colossians 2:9-10", "Colossians 2:14-15", "Colossians 3:1-3", "Hebrews 2:3", "James 1:18", "James 1:23-25", "1 Peter 1:3", "1 Peter 2:2-5"],
    "reflect": "When you look at the gospel, do you see a shop window of things you hope to earn one day, or a mirror showing you who you already are in Christ?"
  },
  {
    "id": "ch2-man-began-in-god",
    "n": 2,
    "title": "Man Began in God",
    "big": "You did not begin in your mother's womb but in God's mind, and Jesus came to reveal and redeem that original design, buying the whole 'field' of humanity because he alone knew the treasure hidden in it.",
    "summary": "The chapter argues that human beings are God's original idea, known before they were formed (Jeremiah 1:5) and designed as partners in the friendship of Father, Son and Spirit. Jesus came not to found a religion but to make God's image visible in human form, so that every person might recognise their true origin in him. The parable of the treasure in the field becomes a picture of God, the rightful owner, paying everything to persuade humanity that it belonged to him all along, ransoming us not from the devil but from our own ignorance and fallen mindset. A long series of the book's own expanded renderings of 2 Corinthians 3, James 1, John 1 and 1 Peter 1 drive home that we see ourselves in Christ as in a mirror, that our birth is from above, and that we grow not into completeness but into awareness of it. The chapter ends with a short song, written by a young member of the author's early ministry team, on God's claim over the person he designed.",
    "points": [
      {"head": "Design starts in a mind", "text": "You do not thank the shop assistant for the design of your phone; it began as someone's idea. In the same way you did not begin in your mother's womb; you began in God. We are not our parents' invention, and our short history on earth is not what introduces us to God, since he knew us before he formed us (Jeremiah 1:5)."},
      {"head": "Partners in the triune friendship", "text": "When God imagined humanity, he thought of us as beings whose friendship would delight Father, Son and Spirit forever. Humanity was meant to share in God's triune oneness, with God's image unmasked in human life. You are God's original thought, his greatest idea."},
      {"head": "God's poetry", "text": "Ephesians 2:10 calls us God's workmanship, his <em>poiēma</em>, created in Christ and fully equipped to show his likeness in ordinary life. God has also gone ahead to level the road for us, like servants preparing a highway for a king (Isaiah 40:3-5). We are not Christ-clones but individually crafted, expressing God's image through our own fingerprint, smile and voice."},
      {"head": "God can never be invisible again", "text": "Colossians 1:15 says Jesus is the image of the invisible God, so that everyone may recognise their true origin in him. The incarnation means God can no longer be thought of as invisible. The calendar itself divides history into before and after Christ."},
      {"head": "No superman suit", "text": "The Creator entered the world through a human womb, his 'passport'. Had he arrived in a superman suit, his life, death and resurrection would be irrelevant to us. He came in our frail humanity to prove that God made no mistake when he made you. Hebrews 4:15 shows him tested in every way yet master over sin; his sympathy is not an excuse for a faulty design but a trophy for humanity."},
      {"head": "Lost means owned", "text": "The most labelled sinners were drawn to Jesus because in him they saw the integrity of their own true life and realised their sinful identity was a lie. In all three parables of Luke 15, 'lost' implies ownership: you cannot be lost unless you belong. The lost coin never lost its value, and like Caesar's coin, we bear the image of the one we belong to."},
      {"head": "The owner buys his own field", "text": "In Matthew 13:44, the field is a cultivated farm with a known value. The man finds treasure, hides it, sells everything and buys the whole field, though it looks run-down and overpriced. The book concludes that the buyer is the field's original owner (Psalm 24:1). God did not buy us from the devil, since a thief never gains real ownership; he ransomed us from our own ignorance and the lies we believed about ourselves. The field, humanity, is all he has."},
      {"head": "Law language versus gift language", "text": "Asking 'who paid whom?' is how law-thinking reads the parable, and it cannot grasp gift-language. God swore by himself to show the heirs of promise how unshakeable his resolve is (Hebrews 6:17). The whole gospel aims to persuade us of our original and now redeemed value."},
      {"head": "The scapegoat that ends scapegoating", "text": "1 Peter 1:18-19 says we were ransomed from a futile inherited way of life not with silver or gold, the currency of our religious effort, but with Christ's blood. The book repeats its claim that God spoke the language of our scapegoat mentality one final time to close that system, since Psalm 40:6-7 says he never required sacrifice. As with Adam's garments of skin, the aim was to change how humans saw themselves, not how God saw them."},
      {"head": "Christ in you, the treasure", "text": "The mystery hidden for ages is Christ in you (Colossians 1:26-27). God's love is based on real value, not pity; in the incarnation he 'found' the treasure where it had been hidden all along, in the clay jar of human life (2 Corinthians 4:7). Religion blindfolds people to it (4:4), but every person is a hidden self-portrait of God. Christ is not mainly found in history, the future or even on the page; Scripture mirrors him so he can be unveiled within you."},
      {"head": "The prophets saw the sufferings and the glory", "text": "Prophetic Scripture pointed to the sufferings of Christ and the glory to follow (1 Peter 1:10-11), and the book defines that glory as God's image and likeness restored in humanity. Isaiah 53 and Psalm 22 foresaw the cross centuries ahead. The book adds that the heavens' precise movements show God knew the exact moment he would enter history and the moment the Messiah would die, a claim it does not substantiate here."},
      {"head": "Mirror, not window", "text": "In the book's rendering of 2 Corinthians 3:16-18, whenever anyone returns to the Lord the veil is removed, and we all see ourselves in Christ as in a mirror. The days of window-shopping are over. Gazing at God's blueprint in Jesus, we realise every feature of his image is reflected in us, and the Spirit leads us from an inferior mindset to the glory of our original design. Change, it argues, is not a lifelong gradual climb like Israel's forty years; we cannot become more than we already are in Christ."},
      {"head": "Co-crucified, now co-alive", "text": "Romans 6:11 and Galatians 2:19-20 tell us to count the 'do-it-yourself' self as dead. The book notes that 'I have been crucified with Christ' is in the perfect tense: it happened then and remains true now. God did not redeem a compromised copy of you but rescued the original. The moment rules replace spontaneity, romance is compromised (Romans 7:6)."},
      {"head": "Born from above by the word of truth", "text": "James 1:17-18 says every good gift comes from the Father of lights, with no shadow of change and no hidden agenda; the idea of gift puts reward-language out of business. God chose to give us birth by the word of truth, and like firstfruits we mirror the whole harvest of his work. Romans 8:29 adds that we were shaped in the same mould as the Son, firstborn from the same womb."},
      {"head": "John's prologue as our story", "text": "The book walks through John 1:1-18. The Word was always face to face with God; the incarnation is not Jesus' origin. The Word came to his own, but many did not identify with their true origin. Those who 'receive' him are those who grasp their association with him, and God confirms they are his offspring. Their birth is from God, not from blood lineage or whether their parents wanted them. Then the Word became flesh, and the glory lost in Adam returned. The book notes that the incarnation verse (14) follows the verses about our birth from God (12-13)."},
      {"head": "Defined by your Father", "text": "There is one God and Father of all, who is in all (Ephesians 4:6; Acts 17:24-28); the father of lies is not the father of the human race. 1 Peter 1:14-17, in the book's reading, calls us to hear accurately, to be who we are because the one whose idea we are is holy, and to 'return home' to him wherever we find ourselves, since we are not defined by circumstances. The law addresses humanity in Adam; grace addresses the same humanity in Christ."}
    ],
    "illustrations": [
      {"title": "The mobile phone", "text": "Nobody congratulates the shopkeeper or the assembly plant for a phone's design; it started in a designer's mind. You too started as God's idea, not in a womb or as your parents' invention."},
      {"title": "Your DNA script", "text": "God wrote the roughly three-billion-letter script of your DNA and mirrored it in every one of your cells (the book cites about seventy-five trillion; modern estimates are lower, around thirty trillion). The point is the astonishing care of the Designer."},
      {"title": "The superman suit", "text": "If Jesus had come in a superhero costume, untouched by real human weakness, his life and death would mean nothing for us. His mother's womb was his passport into our real condition."},
      {"title": "Caesar's coin", "text": "Jesus said to give Caesar what bears Caesar's image and God what bears God's image. Humans bear God's image, so they belong to him, just as the lost coin still bore its inscription."},
      {"title": "The treasure in the farmer's field", "text": "Jesus' farming audience would have known what fields were worth. A man who finds hidden treasure could buy a neglected, thorny field for a bargain, yet he pays everything he has. The twist the book draws out is that the buyer was the true owner all along, paying to persuade the field it was his."},
      {"title": "Gold under the maize", "text": "In the ostrich-feather boom of the late 1800s, a young woman was engaged to one of South Africa's richest farmers, but he broke it off because she spoke so freely about God. She went back to the Free State and married a tenant farmer; after seven years (1904) they bought the land, and years later the first gold in the Free State was found on their farm. Her great-granddaughter recalled that she wrote of how the land had only been farmed for maize while the gold was there all along, and that one day ordinary people would discover the gold they carry."},
      {"title": "The ugly duckling", "text": "How long does a swan take to realise it is a swan? The 'ugly duckling' self was an illusion, and whatever supported it died with Christ. It was the reflection in the water that set the swan free."},
      {"title": "Still waters of reflection", "text": "The book suggests Psalm 23:2-3 ('he leadeth me beside the still waters. He restoreth my soul') can be heard as by waters of reflection my soul remembers who I am. This is a devotional reading rather than a translation."},
      {"title": "The four circles", "text": "Not in this chapter's main text but in its notes: the book pictures Christ as the place where God's fullness and our completeness meet, so that whatever is said of him in Colossians 2:9-10 is mirrored in us."},
      {"title": "A song about being begotten", "text": "The chapter closes with a song written by a nineteen-year-old member of the author's 1980s ministry team. Echoing God's questions to Job about who fathered the rain and the dew, it pictures God knowing each person by name, claiming them by design, inviting them to measure the size of his love, and living within the heart as a home without walls."}
    ],
    "words": [
      {"word": "bereshit (Hebrew)", "meaning": "The first word of Genesis, 'in the beginning'. The book breaks it into <em>be</em> (in) and <em>rosh</em> (head) and reads it as 'in the head', so creation begins in God's mind. The <em>rosh</em> root is real, but 'in the head' is an interpretive play rather than the word's normal sense."},
      {"word": "poiēma (Greek)", "meaning": "Workmanship in Ephesians 2:10; the root of our word poem. The book calls us God's poetry, in whom he finds effortless expression of his character."},
      {"word": "proetoimazō (Greek)", "meaning": "To prepare beforehand. The book links it to the Eastern custom of sending people ahead of a king to level the road, so God has prepared a smooth highway for us (Isaiah 40:3-5)."},
      {"word": "agros (Greek)", "meaning": "A cultivated, agricultural field in Matthew 13:44. Because it is farmland, it already had a known, historical value, which makes the buyer's extravagant price all the more striking."},
      {"word": "diabolos (Greek)", "meaning": "The devil, literally one who throws across or slanders. The book links it to the fallen mindset and the lies we believed about ourselves, from which we were ransomed."},
      {"word": "epistrephō (Greek)", "meaning": "To turn back, used in 2 Corinthians 3:16. The book takes it as returning to where we wandered from, as straying sheep return to their Shepherd (Isaiah 53:6; 1 Peter 2:25)."},
      {"word": "anakaluptō (Greek)", "meaning": "To unveil, used of the unveiled face in 2 Corinthians 3:18: the veil is taken off, so every face can see clearly."},
      {"word": "katoptrizomai (Greek)", "meaning": "To look into a mirror or to reflect as a mirror. The book takes it as gazing into a reflection and seeing oneself mirrored in Christ."},
      {"word": "metamorphoō (Greek)", "meaning": "To be transformed. The book reads it as <em>meta</em> (with) plus <em>morphē</em> (form): being 'with form', the image and likeness waking up within us, presented as the opposite of sin as formlessness."},
      {"word": "hamartia (Greek)", "meaning": "Sin. The book derives it from <em>ha</em> (without) and <em>meros</em> (portion or form), so sin is living without one's allotted form, a distorted identity. Most lexicons connect hamartia instead with 'missing the mark'; the 'without form' etymology is the book's own and is not standard."},
      {"word": "eikōn (Greek)", "meaning": "Image, an exact likeness that always assumes a prototype from which it is drawn, not merely something it resembles."},
      {"word": "doxa (Greek)", "meaning": "Glory, linked by the book to <em>dokeō</em> (to think, to seem), so glory is God's opinion or blueprint-thought of us. 'From glory to glory' means from the fading glory of our own achievements and failures to the unfading glory of our original design."},
      {"word": "ek and apo (Greek)", "meaning": "The book notes that 'from glory to glory' uses <em>apo</em> (away from), while 'from faith to faith' uses <em>ek</em>, which it says always points to source or origin. It uses this distinction to argue that faith is sourced in God."},
      {"word": "sustauroō (Greek)", "meaning": "To be crucified together with, in Galatians 2:20. The book notes the perfect tense: crucified with Christ in the past and continuing in that condition now."},
      {"word": "boulomai (Greek)", "meaning": "To will or resolve, in James 1:18. The book takes it as God's affectionate desire and deliberate decision to give us birth."},
      {"word": "apokueō (Greek)", "meaning": "To give birth, used only by James (1:15 and 1:18). The book contrasts sin's passion giving birth to death with God's passion giving birth to authentic life in us."},
      {"word": "alētheia (Greek)", "meaning": "Truth, from a negative prefix plus <em>lanthanō</em> (to be hidden): that which is unhidden. The word of truth is God's unveiled logic."},
      {"word": "ktisma (Greek)", "meaning": "A created thing, which the book says implies the maker's ownership: God holds the copyright on the idea of us."},
      {"word": "pros (Greek)", "meaning": "Towards, face to face, in John 1:1. The Word was always face to face with God."},
      {"word": "ēn and egeneto (Greek)", "meaning": "John uses <em>ēn</em>, the continuous past of <em>eimi</em> (I am), for the Word's eternal existence, but <em>egeneto</em> (became) for the Word becoming flesh (1:14). So the incarnation is not Jesus' origin, as John 8:58 also shows."},
      {"word": "paralambanō and lambanō (Greek)", "meaning": "To receive, take hold of or identify with (John 1:11-12). The book reads 'receiving' Christ as grasping and identifying with our true origin revealed in him, rather than triggering God into action."},
      {"word": "genesthai (Greek)", "meaning": "To become, in John 1:12, an aorist infinitive. The book argues the aorist presents the action as complete, so our grasping is waking up to a birth already completed in the Word rather than a condition for it."},
      {"word": "teknon and didōmi (Greek)", "meaning": "<em>Teknon</em> is child or offspring. <em>Didōmi</em> (to give) is read by the book in John 1:12 as giving back to someone what already belongs to them, so God confirms a sonship that was already ours."},
      {"word": "theaomai (Greek)", "meaning": "To gaze upon, in John 1:14: we beheld his glory, which captivates our gaze."},
      {"word": "charin anti charitos (Greek)", "meaning": "'Grace for grace' in John 1:16, read by the book as grace mirrored in grace: his fullness is the source of our completeness."},
      {"word": "monogenēs and exēgeomai (Greek)", "meaning": "<em>Monogenēs</em> is the only or unique begotten one. <em>Exēgeomai</em>, from which we get exegesis, is read as being officially appointed to lead and to explain: Jesus is the authorised guide who makes the invisible God known."},
      {"word": "hupakoē (Greek)", "meaning": "Usually obedience, from <em>hupo</em> (under) and <em>akouō</em> (to hear). The book reads it as accurate hearing, being under the influence of what one hears."},
      {"word": "suschēmatizō (Greek)", "meaning": "To be conformed to a pattern, from <em>sun</em> and <em>schēma</em>; the old patterns of a redundant system no longer shape us (1 Peter 1:14)."},
      {"word": "hagios (Greek)", "meaning": "Holy, set apart from common use. The book links holiness with wholeness and harmony with God."},
      {"word": "esesthe (Greek)", "meaning": "'You shall be', in 1 Peter 1:16, from <em>eimi</em>. The book notes a textual variant (<em>genesthe</em>, become) and prefers the reading 'be', so the verse invites us to be who we are rather than strive to become."},
      {"word": "anastrephō (Greek)", "meaning": "To turn back or live, in 1 Peter 1:17; the book plays on <em>ana</em> (up) to describe a turning 'downside up', a return to being at home in God."}
    ],
    "questions": [
      {"q": "Who did God pay when he redeemed us?", "a": "Not the devil, the book insists, because a thief never becomes the owner. Like the man who buys his own field, God paid everything to persuade humanity that it belonged to him; the ransom was from our ignorance and fallen thinking. Traditional ransom and substitution views would add that the cross also deals objectively with guilt and justice, not only with our perception."},
      {"q": "Did we exist before birth?", "a": "The book says we existed first as God's thought and were known before being formed (Jeremiah 1:5; Ephesians 1:4). It does not mean souls lived before birth, the Origenist idea condemned in 553, but that each person began in God's intention."},
      {"q": "Does 'receiving' Christ in John 1:12 make us God's children?", "a": "The book argues that receiving means grasping and identifying with a birth already complete in the Word, so God confirms rather than creates sonship. Most evangelical readers take verse 12 to mean God grants the right to become children to those who believe; this is a real point of debate."},
      {"q": "Is spiritual change gradual?", "a": "The book says we do not grow more complete, only more aware of our completeness, and that a lifetime-climb model is what kept Israel in the wilderness. Many traditions would distinguish a finished standing in Christ from a real, ongoing process of growth, which 2 Corinthians 3:18 itself describes as 'from glory to glory'."}
    ],
    "scriptures": ["Genesis 1:26-27", "Deuteronomy 32:18", "Job 38", "Psalm 22", "Psalm 22:27", "Psalm 23:2-3", "Psalm 24:1", "Psalm 40:6-7", "Isaiah 40:3-5", "Isaiah 53:3-7", "Jeremiah 1:5", "Jeremiah 29:11", "Hosea 6:2", "Matthew 13:44", "Luke 15", "Luke 20:24-25", "Luke 24:27-45", "John 1:1-18", "John 3:3-7", "John 8:44", "John 8:58", "John 14:9", "Acts 17:24-28", "Romans 6:11", "Romans 7:6", "Romans 8:29", "2 Corinthians 3:16-18", "2 Corinthians 4:4-7", "Galatians 2:19-20", "Ephesians 2:10", "Ephesians 3:15", "Ephesians 4:6", "Colossians 1:15", "Colossians 1:26-27", "Colossians 2:9-10", "Colossians 3:10", "Hebrews 4:15", "Hebrews 6:17", "Hebrews 8:1-2", "Hebrews 8:10", "James 1:17-18", "James 1:23-25", "1 Peter 1:2-3", "1 Peter 1:10-11", "1 Peter 1:14-19", "1 Peter 2:25", "1 John 5:20"],
    "reflect": "If you truly began as a thought in God's mind, what lie about your origin or worth would you most like to let go of today?"
  },
  {
    "id": "ch3-romance-redeemed",
    "n": 3,
    "title": "Romance Redeemed",
    "big": "Humanity was made for a love-relationship with the Trinity, and in Christ God restored that union before we knew it, so love is who we are and every relationship can be rebuilt on our shared identity in him.",
    "summary": "Starting from the question of the supreme good, the chapter claims that if 2 Corinthians 3:18 is right, then 1 Corinthians 13 describes our true nature, not an impossible ideal. It roots this in the plural 'us' of Genesis 1:26, in our association with Christ before time, and in a long reading of 2 Corinthians 5:14-21, Colossians 1 and Romans 1:16-17 that stresses God's initiative: in Christ all died, all were reconciled, and righteousness is revealed by God's faith. Along the way it offers word studies on righteousness, reconciliation and adoption, the image of the highway in the wilderness, Jesus preparing a place in the Father's house, and the four circles of John 14:20. It then turns to friendship, arguing that we are designed for it, that society's ills stem from a damaged self-image, and that love works by inspiration, not obligation. It closes by listing five ingredients of lasting friendship that frame the next chapters.",
    "points": [
      {"head": "Love is who you are", "text": "The chapter opens with the old question of the supreme good, citing Henry Drummond's classic sermon on love. If 2 Corinthians 3:18 is true and we see ourselves in Christ as in a mirror, then Paul's love poem in 1 Corinthians 13 is not wishful thinking but a description of our true selves. The book paraphrases verses 4-7: love is patient and kind, content, keeps no record of wrongs, delights in truth, protects and never gives up."},
      {"head": "A relational creation", "text": "The plural in Elohim's 'let us make' gives the creation story a relational flavour. God's oneness is not compromised by being a relationship of persons, and humanity is fully included in that oneness (John 14:20). The Bible's central theme is a union between God and humanity with no hint of distance, inferiority, guilt or suspicion. Humanity is, in the book's phrase, the God-kind by design."},
      {"head": "Inseparable association in Christ", "text": "An association existing before the world's foundation places and defines us in Christ. God cannot think of Jesus without thinking of you. The words 'in Christ' unlock the mystery of the ages, and we have badly underestimated how far back that association goes (2 Timothy 1:9; Romans 8:29)."},
      {"head": "Complete in him", "text": "In Christ all the fullness of deity lives in a human body, proving that human life is tailor-made for God (Colossians 1:19; 2:9). Since we are in him, we lack nothing (2:10). Any teaching that leaves people with a sense of lack rather than completeness, the book says, distracts from the truth."},
      {"head": "Found in Christ before lost in Adam", "text": "The book renders Ephesians 1:4 as chosen in Christ before 'the fall of the world' rather than its 'foundation', and calls the fall a falling away in our minds from our identity, the fruit of believing a lie about ourselves (Isaiah 53:6). God located us in Christ before he lost us in Adam. This translation of <em>katabolē</em> is the book's own; virtually all translations read 'foundation'."},
      {"head": "Neither lucky draw nor popular vote", "text": "Our being in Christ is not the result of a Calvinist lottery, nor of our own choice to follow Jesus, because something does not become true by vote. It is God's doing (1 Corinthians 1:30). Missing humanity's inclusion in Christ before they believed, the book argues, misses the point of Jesus' death, descent, resurrection and ascension, and reduces salvation to a fate managed by religion. Jesus entered our deepest darkness and led us out as his trophies (Ephesians 4:8-9; 2:5-6)."},
      {"head": "Loved, not lost", "text": "Ephesians 1:5-6 speaks of adoption, which the book reads not as Western adoption but as a son's coming of age, like a bar mitzvah (Galatians 4:4-6). God greatly favoured us in Christ, so his love for his Son is his love for us. The gospel is not about telling people how lost they are but reminding them how loved they are."},
      {"head": "One died, so all died", "text": "In 2 Corinthians 5:14 Christ's love leaves only one conclusion: when one died for all, all died. Jesus did not die 99 percent or for 99 percent. If the second half of the verse were limited to those who qualify, the first half would have to change too. If all were included in his death, all were included in his resurrection (5:15), so Paul no longer knows anyone by human labels (5:16)."},
      {"head": "Every old label is dead", "text": "Whatever someone was as Jew or Greek, slave or free, Boer, Zulu, Xhosa, British, Indian, Muslim, American, Chinese or Congolese, died with Christ. The 'if' in 2 Corinthians 5:17 follows a 'therefore' and so states a conclusion, not a condition. Jesus revealed not a potential you but the true you."},
      {"head": "Reconciliation is God's doing", "text": "In 2 Corinthians 5:18-20 the whole idea is God's initiative. God was in Christ reconciling the world, not counting trespasses, and the Father was not separated from the Son on the cross; Psalm 22, quoted in the cry of forsakenness, goes on to say God did not hide his face (22:24). Our ministry is to persuade everyone to realise a reconciliation already accomplished, not to tell them to make it happen."},
      {"head": "The divine exchange", "text": "In 2 Corinthians 5:21 the sinless one took on our distortion and we became his righteousness. The book says Jesus did not become a sinner but humanity's official representative, our scapegoat. It also argues that Jesus was bruised not by God but by the people he came to redeem, citing the Greek Septuagint of Isaiah 53:10, which reads that the Lord wished to cleanse him of his wound. Penal substitution advocates read Isaiah 53:10 (Hebrew) as God himself bruising the servant; this is a live debate."},
      {"head": "The righteousness of God revealed", "text": "Romans 1:16-17 says the gospel's power lies in revealing God's righteousness, 'from faith to faith'. The book reads this as God's faith as the source of ours: the gospel is about what God did right, not what Adam did wrong. Habakkuk 2:4 saw that the promise outranks performance, and Habakkuk 3:17-19 rejoices even when every Deuteronomy 28 curse seems to have arrived (Galatians 3:13)."},
      {"head": "Righteousness is a relationship word", "text": "From the Old English 'rightwiseness', righteousness is about being in right relationship. The Greek <em>dikaiosunē</em> speaks of two parties finding likeness in each other, and Hebrew <em>tzedek</em> includes the beam of a balance. Faith-righteousness cannot be yoked with karma or performance-based approval (2 Corinthians 6:14). We were made sons of the Most High (Psalm 82:6) and forgot the Rock that gave us birth (Deuteronomy 32:18)."},
      {"head": "A highway with no chasms", "text": "Isaiah 40:3-5 and Luke 3:4-6 picture every valley raised and every mountain levelled so all flesh sees God's salvation. In the incarnation every excuse to feel distant from God was removed. Jesus 'prepares a place' in the Father's house (John 14:2-3) not by leaving for a distant mansion but by levelling the road through his death, so we can be where he is, face to face with the Father. A slave works in the house; a son is at home there (John 8:35-36)."},
      {"head": "Christ in you, and in the nations", "text": "Colossians 1:20-29 says God reconciled all things through the cross and presents us blameless. The mystery hidden for ages is Christ in you, and the book adds that what is true in the saints is equally true in the nations: Christ, the desire of nations (Haggai 2:7). Paul's aim is to re-align every mind and present everyone complete. Our life is hidden, or kept secret, with Christ in God (Colossians 3:3)."},
      {"head": "Renewed in the spirit of your mind", "text": "Ephesians 4:21-24 says the truth about you is defined in Jesus. Strip off the old identity like an actor's costume. The book insists the human spirit was never contaminated, like a watermark in a banknote; it was the mind that was darkened. Transformation does not mean pushing information from head to heart, but truth rising from the spirit to the mind (John 7:37-38; Isaiah 55:8-11)."},
      {"head": "Designed for friendship", "text": "We are made for friendship, and hostility, distance and loneliness are foreign to our design. The tragedy of the fall was losing our conviction of who we are, so that the senses and changing circumstances became our reference. Crime, addiction, abuse, family breakdown, corruption and war are symptoms, and treating them alone is like sweeping cobwebs without killing the spider. The book, writing in the early 2000s, sees all violence, from terror groups to governments waging war, as the voice of a sick self-image."},
      {"head": "Have-to versus want-to", "text": "Love's law of liberty works through inspiration, not obligation; there is a difference between willpower and willingness. Everyone longs to feel appreciated, and nothing harms a relationship more than being taken for granted. To appreciate is to recognise real value, and Christ's person and work is that reference point. Love can be misunderstood and still win, and every contradiction can confirm the truth rather than distract from it."}
    ],
    "illustrations": [
      {"title": "The supreme good", "text": "Henry Drummond's famous address on 1 Corinthians 13 asked what the noblest thing to desire in a once-only life is. The book answers with a love that has no other agenda than your good."},
      {"title": "An anniversary morning", "text": "While revising the chapter, the author noticed it was 45 years to the day since he met his wife, a personal illustration of how association, being thought of together, comes naturally. In the same way God cannot think of Jesus without thinking of you."},
      {"title": "The 'I am not' tree", "text": "The book renames the tree of the knowledge of good and evil the tree of 'I am not': its fruit is believing a lie about yourself, as Eve did. Elsewhere it calls it the do-it-yourself tree."},
      {"title": "Habakkuk the embracer", "text": "The book notes that Habakkuk's name means embrace, and passes on a Jewish tradition that he was the son of the Shunammite woman to whom Elisha promised she would 'embrace' a son, and whom Elisha later raised by stretching himself face to face over the dead boy (2 Kings 4). This identification is a later rabbinic legend, not something the Bible states."},
      {"title": "The goddess with the scales", "text": "The Greek goddess of justice, Dikē, whose name is the stem of the word for righteousness, is always pictured holding scales. The book uses her, together with the Hebrew idea of a balance-beam and a Talmudic saying about judging one's neighbour in the scale of innocence, to show that righteousness concerns balance and likeness in relationship."},
      {"title": "The Judaean ravines", "text": "The wilderness of Judaea is cut by deep ravines, some over a thousand feet. Luke's promise that every valley will be filled pictures God seamlessly bridging every chasm that made people feel separated from him."},
      {"title": "Four circles", "text": "To picture John 14:20, imagine four circles fitting inside one another: the Father, Jesus in the Father, us in Jesus, and the Spirit in us. Our knowing does not put anyone in place; it just wakes us to the union."},
      {"title": "Gold becomes currency", "text": "Gold does not become gold when it is discovered, but it does become spendable. Likewise, realising our union does not create it, but it makes it useful in daily life."},
      {"title": "The actor's cloak", "text": "Like an actor taking off the costume of a role, we can strip off the false identity that lust and lies tricked us into wearing (Ephesians 4:22). The costume defined a part, never the person."},
      {"title": "The watermark", "text": "The human spirit is like a watermark in a banknote: whatever happens to the surface, the original mark remains. It was the mind, not the spirit, that was darkened."},
      {"title": "Cobwebs and the spider", "text": "Social programmes often clean away cobwebs (crime, addiction, family breakdown) without killing the spider, a lost sense of identity. Real change must address the root."},
      {"title": "He's my brother", "text": "A man carrying a very sick person a long way was asked if the load was too heavy, and replied that he was not heavy, he was his brother. This is the law of liberty: love lifts by want-to, not have-to."},
      {"title": "Voices on friendship", "text": "The chapter cites the novelist Elizabeth Goudge, who likened a person to a work of art that grows more beautiful the more it is admired, and the Roman philosopher Seneca, who taught that one must live for another in order to live well for oneself."}
    ],
    "words": [
      {"word": "paroxunō (Greek)", "meaning": "To be provoked or irritated, in 1 Corinthians 13:5. The book renders it spiteful: love has no sharp edges."},
      {"word": "Elohim (Hebrew)", "meaning": "God, a plural-form noun used with singular verbs. The book takes the plural as pointing to the relational life of Father, Son and Spirit. Many scholars see it as a plural of majesty; Christians have long read Genesis 1:26 in a Trinitarian light."},
      {"word": "adelphos (Greek)", "meaning": "Brother or sibling, from a connective <em>a</em> and <em>delphus</em> (womb): from the same womb. Christ is not ashamed to call us siblings because we share one origin (Hebrews 2:11)."},
      {"word": "theotēs (Greek)", "meaning": "Deity or godhead, in Colossians 2:9. The book notes it is grammatically feminine and says Jesus shows what Father, Son and Spirit are like in human form."},
      {"word": "katoikeō (Greek)", "meaning": "To dwell in, to be at home in. All God's fullness is at home in Jesus' human body."},
      {"word": "exousia (Greek)", "meaning": "Authority, which the book derives from <em>ek</em> (out of) and <em>eimi</em> (I am): authority flowing from 'I am'. Christ is the authority of our true identity."},
      {"word": "katabolē (Greek)", "meaning": "Normally 'foundation' of the world. The book renders it 'fall' of the world in Ephesians 1:4, a minority reading most scholars reject."},
      {"word": "katenōpion (Greek)", "meaning": "Right before, in the presence of. The book stresses the closest possible proximity: face to face."},
      {"word": "huiothesia (Greek)", "meaning": "Adoption or placing as a son. The book reads it as a coming of age into sonship, sealed by the Spirit crying Abba (Galatians 4:4-6)."},
      {"word": "charitoō (Greek)", "meaning": "To favour greatly, to endue with grace, in Ephesians 1:6; the only other New Testament use is Luke 1:28, of Mary."},
      {"word": "sunechō (Greek)", "meaning": "To press together, hold or constrain, in 2 Corinthians 5:14. The book links it to embracing and resonating: Christ's love grips us and echoes in us."},
      {"word": "ara (Greek)", "meaning": "Therefore, a word of conclusion: 'then all died'."},
      {"word": "metanoia (Greek)", "meaning": "Usually repentance. The book reads it as <em>meta</em> (with) and <em>noeō</em> (perceive): a waking of the mind to what is true, a co-knowing, as in Paul's realisation in 2 Corinthians 5:16. It contrasts this with the Latin <em>paenitentia</em>, behind 'penance'."},
      {"word": "katallassō (Greek)", "meaning": "To reconcile, from the idea of exchanging coins of equal value. God exchanged equal value to restore us to himself, showing our worth."},
      {"word": "kosmos (Greek)", "meaning": "World; in 2 Corinthians 5:19 the book takes it as the whole human family."},
      {"word": "parakaleō (Greek)", "meaning": "To call alongside, urge, plead or comfort. The book links <em>kaleō</em> to calling by name: God's ambassadors call people by their true name. In Luke 15:28 the father pleads with the elder son."},
      {"word": "poieō / 'made' (Greek)", "meaning": "In 2 Corinthians 5:21 'made to be sin' uses <em>poieō</em>; the book connects it with <em>poiēma</em> and renders it as prophetic poetry: Christ acted out humanity's distortion as its scapegoat. This is the book's own rendering."},
      {"word": "chul (Hebrew)", "meaning": "In Deuteronomy 32:18, 'the God that formed thee' (KJV). The book notes the root can mean to whirl or dance and renders it 'the God who danced with you'. The root also means to writhe in birth, which is how most translations understand it here."},
      {"word": "proorizō (Greek)", "meaning": "To mark out beforehand, like an architect's detailed plan (Romans 8:30)."},
      {"word": "dikaiosunē (Greek)", "meaning": "Righteousness, from <em>dikē</em>; the book calls it a relationship word describing two parties finding likeness and harmony with each other."},
      {"word": "heterozugeō (Greek)", "meaning": "To be unequally yoked (2 Corinthians 6:14). The book notes that a rabbi's yoke meant his teaching, so faith-righteousness cannot share a yoke with performance-based teaching."},
      {"word": "tzedek (Hebrew)", "meaning": "Righteousness, which the book links with the beam of a pair of scales: balance and fairness."},
      {"word": "hetoimazō (Greek)", "meaning": "To prepare, especially to level a road for a king. In Luke 3:4 it is an urgent imperative, and in John 14:2-3 it describes Jesus preparing the way for us."},
      {"word": "tribos (Greek)", "meaning": "A worn track or rut. The book sees humanity's well-worn paths of tradition and philosophy being straightened."},
      {"word": "euthus (Greek)", "meaning": "Straight; the book connects it with <em>eu</em> (good, beautiful): the old paths are made beautiful."},
      {"word": "pharanx (Greek)", "meaning": "A ravine or chasm hedged by cliffs (Luke 3:5): every chasm of separation is bridged."},
      {"word": "horaō (Greek)", "meaning": "To see, perceive with the mind (Luke 3:6): all flesh will see God's salvation. The book cites A.T. Robertson's comment that this suits Luke's universal gospel."},
      {"word": "ontōs (Greek)", "meaning": "Truly, really, in John 8:36: free indeed, the opposite of pretence."},
      {"word": "ponēros (Greek)", "meaning": "Evil; the book cites Thayer's lexicon for a sense of hardship and toil, so Colossians 1:21 speaks of a mindset that led into a life of hard labour, and it renames the tree 'good and hard labour'."},
      {"word": "apokatallassō (Greek)", "meaning": "To reconcile fully, restore completely to original value (Colossians 1:21)."},
      {"word": "teleios (Greek)", "meaning": "Perfect, complete, fully efficient (Colossians 1:28)."},
      {"word": "apokalupsis (Greek)", "meaning": "Revelation or unveiling (1 Corinthians 1:7). The book objects to translations that push this unveiling into the future."},
      {"word": "anengklētos (Greek)", "meaning": "Blameless, unaccusable (1 Corinthians 1:8). The book reads it as vindicated in one's identity."},
      {"word": "kaleō (Greek)", "meaning": "To call or name. The book often renders it 'surname': God has given us his family name (1 Corinthians 1:9)."},
      {"word": "ekklesia (Greek)", "meaning": "Church or assembly, from <em>ek</em> (out of) and <em>kaleō</em> (call). The book reads it as the revelation of humanity's original identity (Ephesians 3:9-11)."}
    ],
    "questions": [
      {"q": "Why does the book reject both Calvinist election and 'choosing Jesus' as the basis of salvation?", "a": "Because both make inclusion depend on something other than what God did in Christ for everyone: a secret selection, or a human vote. The book holds that we are in Christ by God's doing, and faith wakes us up to it. Reformed and Arminian Christians would each reply that Scripture links being 'in Christ' to election or to faith; this is one of the book's most debated claims."},
      {"q": "Did God crush Jesus on the cross?", "a": "The book says Jesus was wounded by the humanity he came to save, not by God, and points to the Septuagint of Isaiah 53:10. Penal substitution, held by many evangelicals, reads the Hebrew of that verse as God bruising his servant. The book's view is closer to Christus Victor and participatory readings."},
      {"q": "What does 'I go to prepare a place for you' mean?", "a": "The book reads it as Jesus levelling the road through his death and resurrection so we can be where he is, in the Father's embrace, now. More traditional readings see a future dwelling as well; the two need not exclude each other."},
      {"q": "What are the five ingredients of lasting friendship?", "a": "The chapter ends by listing them: the agape agenda of unconditional love, uncluttered belief, innocence (forgiveness without suspicion or blame), oneness of mind through a shared reference point, and a long-term, sustained 'happily ever after'. These shape the following chapters."}
    ],
    "scriptures": ["Genesis 1:26-27", "Genesis 1:31", "Deuteronomy 28", "Deuteronomy 32:5-6", "Deuteronomy 32:18", "2 Kings 4", "Psalm 22:1", "Psalm 22:24", "Psalm 22:27", "Psalm 22:31", "Psalm 36:9", "Psalm 82:6-7", "Isaiah 40:3-5", "Isaiah 52:10", "Isaiah 52:14-15", "Isaiah 53:4-6", "Isaiah 53:10", "Isaiah 55:8-11", "Habakkuk 2:4", "Habakkuk 3:17-19", "Haggai 2:6-7", "Luke 1:28", "Luke 3:4-6", "Luke 15:28-31", "John 1:1-2", "John 1:18", "John 7:37-38", "John 8:35-36", "John 14:2-3", "John 14:20", "Acts 17:28", "Acts 17:31", "Romans 1:16-17", "Romans 3:23-24", "Romans 4:25", "Romans 6:11", "Romans 8:29-30", "Romans 12:18", "1 Corinthians 1:4-15", "1 Corinthians 1:30", "1 Corinthians 13:4-7", "1 Corinthians 13:12", "2 Corinthians 3:18", "2 Corinthians 5:14-21", "2 Corinthians 6:14-15", "Galatians 2:19-20", "Galatians 3:13", "Galatians 4:4-6", "Ephesians 1:3-6", "Ephesians 2:5-6", "Ephesians 2:10", "Ephesians 3:9-11", "Ephesians 4:7-9", "Ephesians 4:21-24", "Ephesians 6:12", "Colossians 1:13-29", "Colossians 2:9-10", "Colossians 2:14-15", "Colossians 3:1-3", "2 Timothy 1:9", "Titus 2:11", "Hebrews 2:11", "Hebrews 12:2", "James 3:9", "1 Peter 1:3"],
    "reflect": "Who in your life have you been 'knowing from a human point of view', and what would change if you saw them as already included in Christ?"
  },
  {
    "id": "ch4-unconditional-love",
    "n": 4,
    "title": "Unconditional Love: Agape Is the Only Agenda",
    "big": "God loved us first, while we were hostile, and Jesus came not to change God's mind about us but to change our minds about God.",
    "summary": "This short chapter takes up the first ingredient of friendship: unconditional love. It contrasts the gospel's love initiative with religion's karma-economy, which needs paying, returning customers and ever more costly sacrifices to soothe moody gods. Through Romans 5:8-10, Abraham and Isaac on Mount Moriah, and the Ethiopian official reading Isaiah 53, it argues that God provides the Lamb himself and that his love did not increase when we were reconciled; we are simply free to see how much he always loved us. It celebrates God's endless thoughts towards us (Psalms 8 and 139) and his singing delight (Zephaniah 3:17). It ends with practical counsel: be gentle with others, remember who we once were, and become 'pop-ups' of God's kindness in society.",
    "points": [
      {"head": "He loved us first", "text": "What sets true Christianity apart from every religion is God's own initiative to reconcile himself with humanity (1 John 4:19). Grace takes karma out of the equation. Religion depends on paying, returning customers."},
      {"head": "Changing our minds, not God's", "text": "Jesus did not come to talk an angry God into being nice; he came to persuade an indifferent and hostile humanity of God's goodness. He shocked his hearers by saying no one truly knows the Father except through the Son, and that seeing him was seeing the Father. True faith is not humans trying to manage the mood of a bad-tempered deity."},
      {"head": "Hostility did not reduce his love", "text": "Romans 5:10 says we were reconciled while we were enemies. Our hostility did not shrink God's love; he saw equal value in us when he exchanged his Son's life for ours. Now that reconciliation is complete, his life in us saves us completely."},
      {"head": "God provides the Lamb", "text": "The gospel is not about a sacrifice we bring but the Lamb God gives (John 1:29). When Isaac asked where the lamb was, Abraham answered that God would provide it, and then saw a ram caught in a thicket behind him (Genesis 22:7-13). Jesus said Abraham rejoiced to see his day (John 8:56-58)."},
      {"head": "Get to the point of Scripture", "text": "When the Ethiopian official asked Philip who Isaiah 53 was about, Philip began from that very passage and told him the good news of Jesus (Acts 8:30-35). The author once wrote beside that verse a reminder to himself: when you study or teach Scripture, get to the point, which is Jesus (Luke 24:27, 44-45; John 5:39-40)."},
      {"head": "He does not love you more now", "text": "Romans 5:8-9 shows the extremity of God's love: Christ died for us when we were rotten to the core. God does not love us more now that we are reconciled; we are just free to realise how much he loved us all along. If sin could separate us from God's love, redemption would be impossible."},
      {"head": "Equally Immanuel to everyone", "text": "God is not nearer to some than to others; in the incarnation he is equally 'God with us' to every person. God delights in humanity, and his estimate of us outweighs anything that could disqualify us. In Christ he broke down every wall of hostility and every excuse for feeling distant."},
      {"head": "One measure, one worth", "text": "Ephesians 4:7 says grace is given according to the measure of Christ's gift. The book reads this as one measure and one worth for all: our value is defined by his gift, not by rewards for our behaviour (1 Peter 1:18-19)."},
      {"head": "God's mind is full of you", "text": "Job 7:17 and Psalm 8:4 ask what humans are that God should set his heart on them. Psalm 139:17-18 says his thoughts towards us outnumber the sand. It will take eternity to count them. Isaiah 53:11 says he will see the fruit of his suffering and be satisfied."},
      {"head": "God sings and dances over you", "text": "Zephaniah 3:17 pictures God rejoicing over his people with gladness and loud singing; the book adds the image of God spinning round in joyful dance."},
      {"head": "No need to be the macho man", "text": "Paul's counsel not to be quarrelsome (Titus 3:2; 2 Timothy 2:24) means you don't have to win every argument. You don't need to wait for people to change before being kind to them, and there is a big difference between fake politeness and genuine courtesy."},
      {"head": "Remember who we were", "text": "Titus 3:3-4 reminds us that we too were foolish, stubborn, enslaved to cravings, bored, lonely, disliking ourselves and others. The only difference now is what we know to be true about ourselves, since God's kindness and love for humanity has dawned. God says he was found by those who were not looking for him (Isaiah 65:1)."},
      {"head": "Be God's pop-ups", "text": "Live God's initiative. Be like a pop-up window of God's kindness in society, making known his patient fondness for people. Love sets faith in motion, because it is easy for love to believe (Galatians 5:6)."}
    ],
    "illustrations": [
      {"title": "The moody ancestor", "text": "In parts of Africa, the book says, a traditional healer will never tell a client that the ancestors are not angry; the moodier the ancestor seems, the more expensive the sacrifice required. Religion everywhere thrives on this logic."},
      {"title": "The ram behind him", "text": "On Mount Moriah, Abraham told Isaac God would provide the lamb, then looked up and saw a ram caught behind him. The book notes 'behind', linking it to Jesus being the same yesterday (Hebrews 13:8) and to John hearing a voice behind him (Revelation 1:10): God's provision was already in place."},
      {"title": "A note in the margin", "text": "Next to Acts 8:35 in his Bible the author wrote a reminder to get to the point when teaching Scripture, as Philip did by preaching Jesus straight from Isaiah 53."},
      {"title": "Pop-ups", "text": "Like the windows that pop up on a computer screen, believers can appear unexpectedly in everyday life as reminders of God's kindness."}
    ],
    "words": [
      {"word": "katallassō (Greek)", "meaning": "Reconciliation in Romans 5:10: a mutual exchange of equal value, like exchanging coins of equivalent worth (Thayer's lexicon). God saw equal value in us when he gave his Son."},
      {"word": "amachos (Greek)", "meaning": "Not quarrelsome (Titus 3:2), from a negative particle and <em>machē</em>, a fight. The book renders it as not being a brawler or a 'macho man'."},
      {"word": "agapē (Greek)", "meaning": "The New Testament's word for self-giving love, which the chapter calls God's only agenda: a love with no aim except our good."}
    ],
    "questions": [
      {"q": "Did the cross change God's attitude towards us?", "a": "The book says no: Jesus came to change our minds about God, not God's mind about us, and God loved us while we were enemies (Romans 5:8-10). Many evangelicals would say the cross satisfied God's justice and so changed our legal standing before him, while still agreeing that the cross flows from love that was already there."},
      {"q": "Does God love believers more than unbelievers?", "a": "No. God is equally Immanuel to every person and does not love us more once we are reconciled; we are only freer to know his love."},
      {"q": "How should this shape the way we treat difficult people?", "a": "Don't wait for them to change before being kind, avoid needing to win, and remember that we were once just as lost. The only difference is what we now know about ourselves."}
    ],
    "scriptures": ["Genesis 22:7-13", "Job 7:17", "Psalm 8:4", "Psalm 139:17-18", "Isaiah 53:3-7", "Isaiah 53:11", "Isaiah 65:1", "Zephaniah 3:17", "Luke 24:27", "Luke 24:44-45", "John 1:29", "John 5:39-40", "John 8:56-58", "John 14:9", "Acts 8:30-35", "Romans 4:25", "Romans 5:8-10", "Galatians 5:6", "Ephesians 4:7", "Colossians 2:14", "2 Timothy 2:24", "Titus 2:11", "Titus 3:2-4", "Hebrews 13:8", "1 Peter 1:18-19", "1 John 4:19", "Revelation 1:10"],
    "reflect": "Is there any part of you still trying to keep a 'moody god' happy, and what would it feel like to believe he has never loved you less than he does right now?"
  },
  {
    "id": "ch5-uncluttered-belief",
    "n": 5,
    "title": "Uncluttered Belief",
    "big": "Faith is not something we manufacture to move God; it is our echo of God's own faith, his unshakeable conviction about us shown in Jesus, which our unbelief cannot cancel.",
    "summary": "The chapter treats the second ingredient of friendship, uncluttered belief. It argues that our redemption rests on what God believes, not on what we believe: human unbelief cannot cancel God's faithfulness (Romans 3:3-4; 2 Timothy 2:13), and God has sworn by himself to put his purpose beyond dispute (Hebrews 6:13-20). Jesus is God's final 'yes' to humanity, planned before time and announced through all the prophets. Abraham becomes the model of a person who borrowed God's conviction until it became his own, with Isaac's birth and the resurrection as proof that God's faith brings life from death. The chapter then explores how faith speaks and sings even in hardship (Psalm 116; 2 Corinthians 4), and ends by saying people are coins bearing God's image, so true worship means treating every person as you would treat Jesus.",
    "points": [
      {"head": "Love fuels belief", "text": "The same God who engineered the sunrise engineered our salvation. Humanity's redemption is not in dispute because God guarantees it himself. Our unbelief does not change what God believes, since he cannot deny himself (2 Timothy 2:13)."},
      {"head": "Unbelief cannot cancel God's faith", "text": "Romans 3:3-4 asks whether some people's unfaithfulness cancels God's faithfulness, and answers no. Truth is defined in God and is neither threatened nor proven by human experience; it is not decided by popular vote. David's sin did not cancel God's promise to his house (2 Samuel 7:15-16), and we can do nothing against the truth (2 Corinthians 13:8)."},
      {"head": "God swore by himself", "text": "In human affairs we swear by a higher authority to end all dispute. Having no one greater, God swore by himself to Abraham, making the promise as certain as God is (Hebrews 6:13-17). Two unchangeable things, the promise and its fulfilment in Jesus, make it impossible to prove God wrong, so our hope is an anchor already fastened behind the veil, where Jesus went ahead as our forerunner and High Priest (6:18-20)."},
      {"head": "No middleman with Abraham", "text": "The book reads Galatians 3:20 to mean that the covenant with Abraham had no human go-between: it was God alone. The law of Moses, an arrangement with parts for both sides, needed mediators; the grace covenant pointed to Jesus, in whom God fulfilled humanity's part himself. The Word is the promise; the incarnate, crucified and risen Christ is the proof."},
      {"head": "Not bought from the devil", "text": "Again the book insists God did not buy us back from the devil, and Jesus did not die to change the Father's mind. Our minds needed persuading. God was not the one needing reconciliation; he was in Christ reconciling the world to himself (2 Corinthians 5:18-20)."},
      {"head": "God's final yes", "text": "Jesus is God's eternal and final yes to humanity, the fulfilment of every promise (2 Corinthians 1:18-22). There is no maybe in God. Paul and his companions preached Christ <em>in</em> their hearers, not merely to them (Galatians 1:16; Colossians 1:27). God's anointing and seal authorise our oneness with others."},
      {"head": "Planned before time began", "text": "Titus 1:2-3 says God, who cannot lie, promised eternal life before the ages began and revealed it at the right time. Union with God was the original thought behind creation. Then 'the fullness of time' came (Galatians 4:4) and the Word became flesh. Jesus is the same yesterday, today and forever (Hebrews 13:8), so his history outranks anything in our past, present or future, and no ground remains to condemn humanity (Romans 8:1, 34)."},
      {"head": "Jesus is God's language", "text": "Hebrews 1:1-3 says God spoke in fragments through the prophets but has now spoken in a Son. Jesus is the sum of everything God ever said to us, the radiance of his glory and the exact expression of his being. Having made purification for sins, he sat down, and his throne, the book says, is proof of humanity's redeemed innocence. The conversation whispered in prophecy, carved in stone and written in conscience became a human life."},
      {"head": "Circumstances do not contradict the word", "text": "Paul in chains says God's word is not chained (2 Timothy 2:9). His ministry is measured by the word, not his circumstances. We were included in Christ's death and so in his resurrection (2:11). If we contradict ourselves, behaving unlike who we are, God will contradict us and prove us wrong (2:12-13)."},
      {"head": "Abraham borrowed God's conviction", "text": "Abraham had every reason to doubt, yet he kept speaking God's opinion (Romans 4:20-22). His very name was a confession of the promise, and he never shortened it when nothing seemed to change. Every time Sarah called him, she spoke the promise too. God's persuasion rubbed off on Abraham and became his own, and that was counted as righteousness."},
      {"head": "Written for us, sealed by resurrection", "text": "'It was counted to him' was written for our sake too (Romans 4:23-24). Isaac's birth from Sarah's barren womb foreshadowed Jesus rising from the tomb, showing the flesh cannot produce life by effort. Jesus was handed over because of our failures and raised because we were declared righteous (4:25). If humanity were still guilty after his death, his resurrection would be pointless."},
      {"head": "God's faith is the fountain of ours", "text": "We don't need to manufacture our own faith when we can have God's; we only need to know what God believes. Likewise the joy of the Lord is our strength (Nehemiah 8:10, which the book loosely attributes to Paul), and his joy must be based on something true about us: we are his joy. Our doctrines may label us, but only what God knows about us in Christ defines us. Agree with God about you."},
      {"head": "Jesus is the benchmark", "text": "Anything we believe about God that is unlike Jesus is not true of God. The book agrees with Steve McVey that trying to understand God apart from Jesus is mythology, not theology. The Bible is about Jesus, and Jesus is about us: whatever is true of him is true of us, as he is so are we in this world (Ephesians 4:21; 1 John 4:17)."},
      {"head": "Designed to believe and speak", "text": "Because humans are the God-kind, we are made to be persuaded; we are faith-compatible. With David we say, I believed, therefore I spoke (2 Corinthians 4:13; Psalm 116:10). When the soul wants to bolt like a wild horse, David tells it to return to its rest because the Lord has dealt kindly (Psalm 116:7). Sabbath celebrates perfection, and God does not use circumstances to teach us; the finished work of Christ teaches us."},
      {"head": "Contradiction becomes confirmation", "text": "In 2 Corinthians 4:8-9 Paul is hemmed in but not crushed, struck down but not destroyed. The book reads it as being cornered outside but having unlimited space within. Every contradiction becomes a chance to confirm the truth, and realised truth makes faith a fortress. Paul is not a hired career preacher; he is employed by persuasion (1 Corinthians 9:17)."},
      {"head": "Every person bears the image", "text": "Caesar's image made a metal disc legal tender; the gospel reveals God's image in the face of Christ as the 'currency' that redeems humanity, mirroring every person. James warns that we bless God and curse people made in his likeness (James 3:9). The point is not what someone did to deserve the insult but who they are by design. True worship is touching someone's life with the care you would give Jesus."}
    ],
    "illustrations": [
      {"title": "The oath that ends all argument", "text": "When people want to settle a dispute, they swear by someone greater. God, having no one greater, swore by himself, the strongest guarantee possible."},
      {"title": "Two witnesses", "text": "Jesus cited the law's rule that two witnesses establish a matter (John 8:17; Deuteronomy 17:6; 19:15). The book applies it to God's promise and its fulfilment in Jesus: two unchangeable facts settle the case."},
      {"title": "The anchor behind the veil", "text": "Hope is pictured as an anchor already fastened in God's innermost presence, beyond the temple veil, where Jesus has gone ahead of us (Hebrews 6:19-20)."},
      {"title": "Abraham's name", "text": "Abraham means 'father of a multitude'. He did not shorten it to 'Abe' when nothing seemed to change; every introduction declared the promise. Sarah probably spoke it most, and each time they addressed each other they were speaking the promise."},
      {"title": "Dressed in persuasion", "text": "Abraham wore his conviction like everyday clothes or armour, covered head to toe in it, which is how the book reads the word 'fully persuaded'."},
      {"title": "The wild horse", "text": "In Psalm 116 the soul is pictured as a horse wanting to gallop off into distraction, and David calls it back to rest in God's kindness."},
      {"title": "Paul singing in prison", "text": "Faith speaks and even sings, as Paul and Silas did in the Philippian jail (Acts 16:25). Let faith break out into song."},
      {"title": "Caesar's coin and the lost coin", "text": "The image stamped on a coin makes it legal tender. God's image in Christ, mirrored in every person, is what makes humanity his, and the lost coin never lost its value."}
    ],
    "words": [
      {"word": "peras (Greek)", "meaning": "End or limit (Hebrews 6:16): the point beyond which dispute cannot go."},
      {"word": "mesiteuō (Greek)", "meaning": "To mediate or guarantee, from <em>mesos</em> (middle). God placed himself in the middle of his creation in the incarnation and pledged himself with an oath (Hebrews 6:17)."},
      {"word": "aiōnios (Greek)", "meaning": "Of the ages. The book describes eternity as divided into ages, the shorter contained in the longer, and says God's purpose for us came before them all (Titus 1:2)."},
      {"word": "chronos and kairos (Greek)", "meaning": "<em>Chronos</em> is measured time or duration; <em>kairos</em> is a due, specific moment. God's plan preceded calendar time and arrived at its proper moment."},
      {"word": "palai (Greek)", "meaning": "Of old, long ago (Hebrews 1:1). The book links it to <em>palin</em> (again), so the ancient word falls fresh like dew (Deuteronomy 32:2)."},
      {"word": "eschatos (Greek)", "meaning": "Last or extreme, the final conclusion (Hebrews 1:2). The book says revealed and redeemed sonship is what defines eschatology."},
      {"word": "eklegomai / eklektos (Greek)", "meaning": "Chosen or elect (2 Timothy 2:10). The book derives it from <em>ek</em> (source) and <em>legō</em> / <em>logos</em> (word), and reads it as the life of our design. This is the book's own interpretive gloss rather than a standard meaning."},
      {"word": "arneomai (Greek)", "meaning": "To deny, which the book renders 'contradict' (2 Timothy 2:12): behaving unlike who we really are."},
      {"word": "plērophoreō (Greek)", "meaning": "To be fully persuaded (Romans 4:21). The book breaks it into 'to be completely covered' and <em>phoreō</em> (to wear clothes or armour): Abraham wore his conviction like a garment. Standard lexicons connect it with 'carrying fully', so the clothing picture is a sermonic image."},
      {"word": "logizomai (Greek)", "meaning": "To reckon, count or calculate (Romans 4:22; 6:11): a calculation that leads to only one logical conclusion. Abraham counted his own body as dead; we count ourselves dead to sin."},
      {"word": "dia (Greek)", "meaning": "Because of. In Romans 4:25 the same word appears twice: handed over because of our failures, raised because of our being declared righteous."},
      {"word": "chriō (Greek)", "meaning": "To smear or rub with oil, to anoint (2 Corinthians 1:21)."},
      {"word": "echō (Greek)", "meaning": "To have or hold. The book links it to echo and embrace: we hold and echo the same spirit of faith (2 Corinthians 4:13)."},
      {"word": "aman (Hebrew)", "meaning": "To believe, behind 'I believed' in Psalm 116:10, with root ideas of supporting, nurturing like a parent, being firm and certain; also the source of 'amen'. The book connects it with going to the right hand, picturing faith relocating us to where we are seated with Christ."},
      {"word": "dabar (Hebrew)", "meaning": "To speak; the book says it means arranging words so that their effect subdues, like a lawyer's letter."},
      {"word": "anah (Hebrew)", "meaning": "In Psalm 116:10, usually translated 'afflicted'. The book notes the same root (a homonym in many lexicons) can mean to answer, speak up or sing, and prefers 'I will sing aloud' in context."}
    ],
    "questions": [
      {"q": "Does my unbelief cancel what God has done?", "a": "No. Romans 3:3-4 and 2 Timothy 2:13 say human unfaithfulness cannot cancel God's faithfulness. The book takes this to mean redemption is secure for everyone; others would say it guarantees God's promises while leaving individuals responsible to trust them."},
      {"q": "Where does faith come from?", "a": "From God's own faith. We do not have to generate belief; we only need to know what God believes about us, which is shown in Jesus, and agree with him."},
      {"q": "Does God use hardship to teach us lessons?", "a": "The book says God does not use circumstances to teach us; the finished work of Christ is our teacher. Many Christians would point to passages such as Hebrews 12 and James 1 on God's discipline and trials, so this is a debated emphasis."},
      {"q": "Why does it matter how I speak to and about others?", "a": "Because every person bears God's image. Cursing a human made in his likeness while praising God is a contradiction, and true worship is treating each person as you would treat Jesus."}
    ],
    "scriptures": ["Genesis 1:31", "Genesis 2:2", "Genesis 22:7-13", "Deuteronomy 17:6", "Deuteronomy 19:15", "Deuteronomy 32:2", "Deuteronomy 32:7", "Deuteronomy 32:18", "2 Samuel 7:15-16", "Nehemiah 8:10", "Psalm 19:1-2", "Psalm 110:1", "Psalm 116:7-10", "Isaiah 53:11", "Habakkuk 2:4", "John 1:14", "John 5:39", "John 8:13-18", "John 10:30", "John 14:3", "John 14:20", "Acts 16:25", "Romans 1:17", "Romans 3:3-4", "Romans 4:19-25", "Romans 6:11", "Romans 8:1", "Romans 8:34", "1 Corinthians 9:17", "2 Corinthians 1:18-22", "2 Corinthians 4:1", "2 Corinthians 4:8-9", "2 Corinthians 4:13", "2 Corinthians 5:18-20", "2 Corinthians 13:5-8", "Galatians 1:16", "Galatians 3:20", "Galatians 4:4", "Ephesians 4:21", "Colossians 1:24", "Colossians 1:27", "1 Thessalonians 2:13", "1 Timothy 4:9", "2 Timothy 2:9-13", "Titus 1:2-3", "Hebrews 1:1-3", "Hebrews 6:13-20", "Hebrews 10:23", "Hebrews 12:2", "Hebrews 13:8", "James 1:18", "James 1:24", "James 3:9", "1 John 2:7-8", "1 John 4:17"],
    "reflect": "What does God believe about you, as shown in Jesus, and where in your life could you simply agree with him today instead of trying to work up faith of your own?"
  },
  {
    "id": "ch6-innocence-redeemed",
    "n": 6,
    "title": "Innocence Redeemed",
    "big": "The cross and resurrection did not just forgive sins but ended sin-consciousness and declared humanity innocent, so we can live free of guilt, accusation and religious performance in face-to-face friendship with God.",
    "summary": "The longest chapter so far takes up innocence, the third ingredient of friendship. It argues that sinners were drawn to Jesus because he showed them their original innocence, and that sin-consciousness, the karma mindset of Simon Peter's 'depart from me', makes real friendship with God impossible. Romans 4:25 is called one of the Bible's most important verses: Jesus died because of our failures and was raised because we were declared righteous. The chapter re-reads repentance as a change of mind rather than penance, and works through Colossians 2-3, Romans 6 and 8, Hebrews 8-10 and other passages to show that the record of guilt has been erased, the accuser disarmed, the sacrificial system closed and God's memory of sin removed. It ends with the stories of Joshua the high priest in Zechariah 3 and Zacchaeus, the 'innocent one', whose name Jesus reminded him of.",
    "points": [
      {"head": "Jesus showed sinners their innocence", "text": "Sinners did not flock to Jesus because he offered a watered-down rule-book. He showed them, as in a mirror, their true origin and original innocence, which their conscience recognised. He told the woman to go and sin no more, not merely to sin less, because he knew something about our design that we had forgotten, and what he revealed he also redeemed."},
      {"head": "Peter's karma reflex", "text": "After a night of catching nothing, Simon seemed comfortable blaming his failure on his sinfulness, because he had been schooled in the law of blessings and curses (Deuteronomy 28). When Jesus gave him a miraculous catch he could take no credit for, he fell back into karma mode and begged Jesus to leave because he was sinful (Luke 5:8). Thankfully Jesus did not pick disciples by performance."},
      {"head": "Sin-consciousness kills friendship", "text": "Sin-consciousness strengthens the law of performance, contradicts grace and leaves no ground for friendship. A relationship built on accusation, inferiority, guilt or suspicion has no future. The innocence of humanity before the fall, the same innocence Jesus enjoys with the Father, is now restored to every person. The prodigal son was not put on probation; he stood fully accepted in his father's love."},
      {"head": "His resurrection is our receipt", "text": "Romans 4:25 (KJV): 'Who was delivered for our offences, and was raised again for our justification.' The book calls it one of the Bible's most important statements: the cross equals our sins, the resurrection equals our innocence. His resurrection is the official receipt of our acquittal; if humanity were still guilty after his death, the resurrection would be irrelevant. Paul told the Athenians God gave proof of this to all by raising Jesus (Acts 17:31)."},
      {"head": "Peace means a dovetail joint", "text": "Romans 5:1 sums up the first four chapters: our innocence has nothing to do with our qualifying; it is entirely Jesus' doing, so let us enjoy face-to-face friendship with God. The book prefers the reading 'let us have peace' and explains peace as joining back together, like a dovetail joint, the strongest joint in carpentry. Romans 5:2 pictures Jesus as God's face-to-face grace embrace of the whole human race."},
      {"head": "Repentance is a change of mind, not penance", "text": "<em>Metanoia</em> means waking up to what is true and realigning one's thinking; it has nothing in common with the Latin <em>paenitentia</em>, behind 'penance', self-inflicted payback. The book says Tertullian already noted that metanoia meant a change of mind, not confession of sins, yet the Latin tradition after Jerome rendered it 'do penance', and guilt-money funded cathedrals and ministries. It adds that Lorenzo Valla's 15th-century critique of the Vulgate flagged the mistranslation but met resistance. Isaiah 55:8-11 shows God's word cancelling the distance between his thoughts and ours, as rain and snow soak the soil and awaken its seed."},
      {"head": "Truth on open display", "text": "Paul renounced hidden agendas and refused to twist God's word, for example by mixing a little law into grace to 'balance' it (2 Corinthians 4:2). Truth is best displayed in human life, commended to everyone's conscience. Our belief in God does not define him; his faith in what he knows about us defines us. Faith is to the spirit what the senses are to the body."},
      {"head": "More than forgiveness", "text": "God overlooked the times of ignorance and now urges all people everywhere to wake up (Acts 17:30-31). God's work in Christ did far more than forgive sins; it broke sin's dominion (Ephesians 4:8-10; Colossians 2:14-15). Romans 8:1 declares no condemnation in Christ; the book notes that the phrase about walking after the Spirit was added there from verse 4 in later manuscripts, probably by someone uneasy with an unconditional declaration. 'In Christ' is the PIN code of the Bible."},
      {"head": "The law could not, so God did", "text": "Romans 8:2-7 says the law was only an instruction manual and could not free us from sin's grip in our bodies. God came in his Son in our very flesh and condemned sin there. Jesus' body was no different from ours, which proves the human frame can master sin (Hebrews 4:15). Thinking patterns follow their reference point: the senses or the Spirit."},
      {"head": "The record of guilt erased", "text": "Colossians 2:11-15 says we were included in Christ's death, our true circumcision, and in his burial and resurrection, which baptism pictures. God graciously restored our innocence and forgave all our lapses. Christ's body on the cross displayed the handwritten record of our guilt, and in dying our death he wiped it out like a grease stain from the conscience. He disarmed every ruling power and accusation, stripping religion of its power to manipulate people with guilt, and made a public spectacle of them."},
      {"head": "Religion's performance pyramid ended", "text": "The book's comment on 1 Corinthians 15:24 says Christ brought to nothing the law of works behind every ranking system, every rule, authority and power based on performance or preference. The rulers of that age had no idea of this hidden wisdom, or they would never have crucified the Lord of glory (1 Corinthians 2:7-8). The cross looks foolish and weak, yet outdoes the wisest human schemes (1 Corinthians 1:22-25). Paul resolved to know nothing among people except Jesus Christ crucified, Christ in them (1 Corinthians 2:2)."},
      {"head": "God's memory was not the problem", "text": "Hebrews 8:10-12 promises laws written on hearts, knowledge of God that no longer rests on religious instruction, and sins remembered no more. God never had a problem with sin-consciousness; we did. He was not hiding from Adam and Eve; they hid from him. Revenge, guilt, shame, suspicion and inferiority are enemies of romance, and neither rules nor willpower could deal with them (Romans 7)."},
      {"head": "The sacrificial system had a sell-by date", "text": "The scapegoat system managed the fallout of sin but could not reach its root; repeated sacrifices actually reinforced the awareness of guilt (Hebrews 10:2-3). We needed rescue from sinfulness, not just forgiveness; the spider, not just the cobwebs. God does not demand a sacrifice; he provides one, going to an embarrassing extreme to convince us of his heart. The book repeats its reading of Adam's garments of skin: for Adam's sake, prefiguring God clothing himself in our skin."},
      {"head": "The finished work in Hebrews 9-10", "text": "Old rituals could not cleanse the conscience and were temporary until the time of 'setting things right' (Hebrews 9:9-10). Christ entered once with his own blood and secured eternal redemption, so our only priestly offering now is thanksgiving, the fruit of our lips. The law was only a sketch, not the finished portrait (10:1); the gospel is news, not prediction. By one offering he perfected for all time those being sanctified, sat down, and declares that he will remember their sins no more (10:10-18). So we draw near with full confidence and gather to remind one another of who we are (10:19-25)."},
      {"head": "Dead to sin, alive to God", "text": "Romans 6:6-18 says our old self was crucified with him, so sin's vehicle was scrapped. Christ died to sin once for all; calculate the cross and conclude that you too are dead to sin and alive to God. You owe sin nothing, and grace, not law, is now your master, which is no licence to sin. One act of righteousness brings acquittal for all people (Romans 5:18)."},
      {"head": "Mind the throne room", "text": "Colossians 3:1-11 invites us to see ourselves raised and seated with Christ and to occupy our minds with that reality. When Christ is revealed, which the book reads as 'every time' he is revealed, we are revealed with him. So put to death lust, greed and idolatry (worshipping a distorted image of yourself), and put away rage, slander and lies, for the old life was a costume foreign to our design. In Christ, identity goes beyond nationality, class or gender."},
      {"head": "All judgment drawn to him", "text": "In John 12:27-33 Jesus calls his hour the culmination of his destiny; the Father's voice speaks for the crowd's sake; the ruler of this world is thrown out. When he is lifted up he draws all to himself, which the book reads as drawing all judgment onto himself, and then, as in Hosea 6:2, raising the humanity he represents. Revelation 12:9-11 shows the accuser cast down and the business of accusing humanity bankrupted by the blood of the Lamb."},
      {"head": "Clean clothes for the accused", "text": "Psalm 103 celebrates a God who does not treat us as our sins deserve and removes them as far as east from west. In Zechariah 3, Satan accuses Joshua the high priest, dressed in filthy clothes, but the Lord rebukes the accuser, removes the filthy garments and clothes him in clean robes and a turban, promising to remove guilt in a single day. Daily life is now a Passover feast of sincerity and truth, innocence without the old leaven (1 Corinthians 5:8)."},
      {"head": "Abide without shame", "text": "1 John 2:28 calls God's children to abide in him so they have confidence and no shame at his appearing. The book reads his <em>parousia</em> as his present nearness rather than mainly a future coming, so we live unashamed in his immediate presence now."}
    ],
    "illustrations": [
      {"title": "Simon's empty nets", "text": "After a fruitless night, Simon was more at ease blaming himself than receiving a gift. When the miraculous catch came, he begged Jesus to leave because he was a sinner. Karma made more sense to him than grace."},
      {"title": "The prodigal not on probation", "text": "The returning son was not given a trial period to prove himself; he was restored at once as a son in his father's love, as if he had never left."},
      {"title": "Losing altitude", "text": "The book pictures a lapse or trespass as a bird that stops flying and loses altitude, a fall from our true height rather than a change in our nature."},
      {"title": "The dovetail joint", "text": "In carpentry the dovetail is the strongest joint. Peace with God is like that: two pieces joined back together so they hold."},
      {"title": "Grease stains on the conscience", "text": "The handwritten record of guilt and hurtful memories were like grease stains on the conscience, and God wiped them out completely."},
      {"title": "Paraded through the streets", "text": "At the cross God stripped the spiritual tyrants of their sham authority and led them like defeated captives in a public triumph."},
      {"title": "The little princess heifer", "text": "The red heifer of the old sacrifices was the costliest offering, often a spotless calf raised like a family pet. Even this best of the law's offerings brought only temporary relief, showing that no animal sacrifice could cleanse the conscience."},
      {"title": "The carpenter's chalk sketch", "text": "Quoting Albert Barnes's commentary, the book compares the law to a rough chalk outline or architect's sketch: it resembles the real thing but cannot shelter you like a finished house or satisfy like a finished portrait."},
      {"title": "Ears or a body?", "text": "Hebrews 10:5 quotes Psalm 40 as 'a body hast thou prepared me', while the Hebrew reads 'mine ears hast thou opened'. The book passes on Adam Clarke's report of Benjamin Kennicott's conjecture that the Hebrew letters were miscopied; scholars more often explain it as the Greek translators' interpretive rendering."},
      {"title": "The actor's garments", "text": "An actor's costume defines his role in the play but not who he is. The old life was a costume we have now taken off (Colossians 3:9)."},
      {"title": "A diamond in the sun", "text": "Sincerity in 1 Corinthians 5:8 is pictured as a diamond held up to the sun to show it has no flaws: our innocence can stand scrutiny."},
      {"title": "Joshua's filthy clothes", "text": "In Zechariah 3 the accuser stands beside Joshua the high priest in his filthy garments, but God silences the accuser and has him dressed in clean robes, a picture of humanity's guilt removed in a single day."},
      {"title": "Zacchaeus, Mr Innocent", "text": "In Jericho, a rich chief tax collector kept trying to see Jesus and climbed a sycamore. Jesus called him down and invited himself home, and the crowd grumbled. Zacchaeus defended himself, saying he gave half to the poor and repaid four times anyone who accused him. Jesus moved the conversation from accusation to their names: Jesus means salvation, and Zacchaeus means pure or innocent. Salvation had come, and he too was a son of Abraham by faith (Luke 19:1-10)."},
      {"title": "Ishmael and mixed messages", "text": "The book says Ishmael stands for the flesh's clumsy attempt to compete with faith, and so for preaching a mixed message of law and grace, rather than for any particular religion (Galatians 3:7; 4)."}
    ],
    "words": [
      {"word": "paraptōma (Greek)", "meaning": "Trespass or offence (Romans 4:25; Colossians 2:13), from <em>para</em> (beside) and <em>piptō</em> (to fall). The book links <em>piptō</em> to <em>petomai</em> (to fly), so a trespass is to stop flying and lose altitude, a fallen mindset. The flight link is the book's own image."},
      {"word": "dikaiōthentes (Greek)", "meaning": "'Having been justified' (Romans 5:1), an aorist participle: our acquittal is a completed act, entirely Christ's doing."},
      {"word": "echōmen (Greek)", "meaning": "'Let us have' peace (Romans 5:1), a subjunctive found in many early manuscripts, versus <em>echomen</em> (we have). The book follows the 'let us have/enjoy' reading as an invitation to engage the union. Both readings are argued for by textual scholars."},
      {"word": "eirēnē (Greek)", "meaning": "Peace, which the book connects to <em>eirō</em> (to join): being set at one again, like a dovetail joint, a place of friendship beyond guilt, suspicion or inferiority."},
      {"word": "metanoia and paenitentia (Greek and Latin)", "meaning": "<em>Metanoia</em> is a change of mind, waking up to what is true. <em>Paenitentia</em> is the Latin rendering behind 'penance' and 'repentance', which the book says shifted the meaning to self-punishment and payback."},
      {"word": "panourgia (Greek)", "meaning": "Craftiness (2 Corinthians 4:2), from <em>pas</em> (all) and <em>ergon</em> (work). The book reads it as wearisome labour of every kind, especially manipulating God's word."},
      {"word": "suneidēsis (Greek)", "meaning": "Conscience, literally knowing together; the book calls it joint seeing and contrasts it with hades as not seeing."},
      {"word": "anthrōpos (Greek)", "meaning": "Human being. The book derives it from <em>ana</em> (upward) and <em>tropos</em> (manner of life), an upward-looking creature; the true etymology is uncertain."},
      {"word": "charizomai (Greek)", "meaning": "To forgive, to grant freely (Colossians 2:13); the book renders it as God graciously restoring our innocence and dignity."},
      {"word": "exaleiphō (Greek)", "meaning": "To wipe out or blot out (Colossians 2:14; Acts 3:19). The book links <em>aleiphō</em> to greasing or staining, so guilt and painful memories are greasy stains wiped clean; Plato used the word of erasing writing."},
      {"word": "cheirographon (Greek)", "meaning": "A handwritten document or certificate of debt (Colossians 2:14): in our own handwriting, the book says, humanity signed its own death sentence."},
      {"word": "dogma (Greek)", "meaning": "Decree or ordinance; the book links it to <em>dokeō</em> (to think) as thought patterns engraved by constant failure under the law."},
      {"word": "apekduomai (Greek)", "meaning": "To strip off, to disarm (Colossians 2:15; 3:9): the religious facade of the law of works was stripped away."},
      {"word": "deigmatizō (Greek)", "meaning": "To make a public example or spectacle (Colossians 2:15)."},
      {"word": "archē, exousia and dunamis (Greek)", "meaning": "Rule, authority and power (1 Corinthians 15:24). The book reads these as every system of ranking people by performance, authority claimed on the basis of what I can do better than you, and the power of willpower, all brought under grace."},
      {"word": "katargeō (Greek)", "meaning": "To abolish or make idle, from <em>kata</em> and <em>argos</em> (idle, without labour): freed from self-effort to improve what God has already perfected."},
      {"word": "krinō (Greek)", "meaning": "To judge or determine (1 Corinthians 2:2), here an aorist: Paul's mind was made up once for all to know people only in the light of Christ crucified."},
      {"word": "mnēmeion (Greek)", "meaning": "Tomb or grave (John 5:28), related to memory. The book reads it as remembrance: those who forgot who they are will hear his voice, as Psalm 22:27 says the ends of the earth will remember and turn to the Lord."},
      {"word": "diorthōsis (Greek)", "meaning": "Reformation or setting right (Hebrews 9:10), used only here in the New Testament: straightening what was crooked, like setting a broken limb."},
      {"word": "damalis (Greek)", "meaning": "Heifer (Hebrews 9:13), linked by the book to <em>damazō</em> (to tame): a tame, cherished young cow, the costliest of the old offerings."},
      {"word": "nekros ergon (Greek)", "meaning": "Dead works (Hebrews 9:14): dead religious routine that cannot compete with the risen Christ in you."},
      {"word": "katechō and anochē (Greek)", "meaning": "The book contrasts <em>katechō</em> (to hold down, Romans 1:18), echoing downward, with <em>anochē</em> (forbearance, Romans 2:4; 3:26), echoing upward, to encourage setting the mind on things above."},
      {"word": "hotan (Greek)", "meaning": "Usually 'when' (Colossians 3:4). The book argues it can mean 'whenever' or 'every time', so Christ's appearing and ours is a repeated present reality rather than only a future event. Most translations keep the future sense."},
      {"word": "orgē (Greek)", "meaning": "Wrath (Colossians 3:6). The book reads it as desire or passion and notes that 'on the children of disobedience' is missing from some manuscripts; textual scholars are divided on that phrase."},
      {"word": "blasphēmia (Greek)", "meaning": "Slander or abusive speech (Colossians 3:8): any attempt to belittle someone or damage their reputation."},
      {"word": "teteleiōken and hagiazomenous (Greek)", "meaning": "'He has perfected' (perfect tense: completed with lasting effect) and 'those being sanctified' (present participle) in Hebrews 10:14. The book stresses the completed, ongoing perfection of one sacrifice."},
      {"word": "ekdechomenos and tethōsin (Greek)", "meaning": "'Waiting' (present participle) and 'be made' (aorist subjunctive) in Hebrews 10:13: the book reads these as a certain outcome flowing from Christ having already sat down."},
      {"word": "ephapax (Greek)", "meaning": "Once for all (Romans 6:10): done with permanent validity, never needing repetition."},
      {"word": "paristēmi (Greek)", "meaning": "To present or place at someone's disposal (Romans 6:13): stand ready for God like someone raised from the dead."},
      {"word": "tupos (Greek)", "meaning": "Form, pattern or mould (Romans 6:17): the teaching we embraced shapes us like a mould."},
      {"word": "ekballō exō (Greek)", "meaning": "Cast out (John 12:31): the book calls it a double expression, completely thrown out, taken out of the equation."},
      {"word": "airō (Greek)", "meaning": "To lift up or take away (John 1:29): the Lamb lifted up our sin and broke its rule."},
      {"word": "archaios and ophis (Greek)", "meaning": "Ancient and serpent (Revelation 12:9). The book links <em>ophis</em> to seeing, so the serpent works through visual illusion; this etymology is the book's suggestion."},
      {"word": "aggelos (Greek)", "meaning": "Messenger or angel. The book connects it to <em>agō</em> (to lead) and <em>agelē</em> (a herd), so 'shepherd-messengers'; the usual derivation is simply 'messenger'."},
      {"word": "katēgōr (Greek)", "meaning": "Accuser (Revelation 12:10), from <em>kata</em> and <em>agora</em> (marketplace): one whose business is public accusation. The book says this business has been bankrupted."},
      {"word": "eilikrineia (Greek)", "meaning": "Sincerity (1 Corinthians 5:8), traditionally explained as tested by the light of the sun, like a flawless diamond."},
      {"word": "Zakkai (Hebrew)", "meaning": "The root of Zacchaeus' name, meaning pure, clean or innocent. Jesus, whose name means 'the Lord saves', pairs salvation with innocence."},
      {"word": "zēteō (Greek)", "meaning": "To seek (Luke 19:3), here in the imperfect: Zacchaeus kept on trying to see Jesus."},
      {"word": "teknion (Greek)", "meaning": "Little child (1 John 2:28), a diminutive: like an infant in its mother's arms."},
      {"word": "parousia (Greek)", "meaning": "Presence or arrival (1 John 2:28). The book insists the root sense is presence and objects that most translations render it 'coming'. Mainstream scholarship holds that in many New Testament passages it does refer to Christ's future coming, so this is strongly debated."}
    ],
    "questions": [
      {"q": "If we are already innocent, why did Jesus tell people to stop sinning?", "a": "Because he showed them who they really were. 'Go and sin no more' is not a demand for better performance but a call to live from the innocence and design he revealed and redeemed. Grace is not a licence to sin (Romans 6:15)."},
      {"q": "What is wrong with feeling sinful?", "a": "The book says sin-consciousness keeps people in a karma mindset, strengthens performance religion and undermines friendship, as with Peter's 'depart from me'. Traditional teaching would stress that honest conviction of sin has a healthy place, so long as it leads to confidence in grace rather than ongoing shame."},
      {"q": "What does repentance really mean?", "a": "A change of mind, waking up to the truth, rather than penance or self-punishment. Most scholars agree metanoia means a change of mind, though many add that in the Bible it includes turning from wrongdoing in life as well as thought."},
      {"q": "Why did God accept sacrifices in the Old Testament if he did not want them?", "a": "The book says the sacrificial system spoke humanity's own language of appeasement to address the effects of sin and to point to its own weakness, so it could finally be closed when God gave himself (Psalm 40:6-8; Hebrews 10:5-10). Many evangelicals would say God instituted sacrifices positively as types of Christ's atoning work."},
      {"q": "Does Romans 8:1 have conditions attached?", "a": "The book notes that 'who walk not after the flesh, but after the Spirit' at the end of 8:1 in the KJV is absent from the earliest manuscripts and was likely copied from verse 4, which modern translations agree with. So the verse is a plain declaration: no condemnation in Christ Jesus."},
      {"q": "What happened in the Zacchaeus story?", "a": "The book reads Zacchaeus' words as a defence against constant accusation rather than a promise to reform, and Jesus responds not with an audit but by naming him: salvation has come, and the innocent one is a son of Abraham. Many readers take his words as a pledge of restitution, a response to grace; both readings are held."}
    ],
    "scriptures": ["Genesis 3:15", "Genesis 3:21", "Deuteronomy 28", "Psalm 22:27", "Psalm 40:6-8", "Psalm 103:1-12", "Psalm 110:1", "Isaiah 1:18", "Isaiah 53:4-5", "Isaiah 53:11", "Isaiah 55:8-11", "Jeremiah 31:33-34", "Hosea 6:2", "Zechariah 3:1-9", "Luke 5:8", "Luke 10:18", "Luke 15:11-32", "Luke 19:1-10", "John 1:29", "John 5:28", "John 8:11", "John 8:39-44", "John 12:27-33", "John 14:20", "John 16:8-11", "Acts 3:19", "Acts 17:30-31", "Romans 4:25", "Romans 5:1-2", "Romans 5:18", "Romans 6:6-18", "Romans 7", "Romans 8:1-7", "1 Corinthians 1:22-25", "1 Corinthians 2:2", "1 Corinthians 2:7-8", "1 Corinthians 5:8", "1 Corinthians 6:19-20", "1 Corinthians 15:21-22", "1 Corinthians 15:24-25", "2 Corinthians 3:18", "2 Corinthians 4:2", "2 Corinthians 4:18", "2 Corinthians 5:14-16", "Galatians 3:7", "Ephesians 1:4", "Ephesians 2:5-6", "Ephesians 4:8-10", "Colossians 2:11-15", "Colossians 3:1-11", "Hebrews 1:3", "Hebrews 4:15", "Hebrews 8:10-12", "Hebrews 9:9-14", "Hebrews 9:26-28", "Hebrews 10:1-25", "1 Peter 1:10-12", "1 Peter 3:18", "1 Peter 4:1-2", "1 John 2:28", "Revelation 12:9-11", "Revelation 13:8"],
    "reflect": "If God has wiped the record clean and the accuser has lost his case, which old accusation are you still letting speak, and what would it be like to hear Jesus call you by your true name instead?"
  }
];
